[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) in AWS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) automatically distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses. It improves the availability and fault tolerance of your applications. AWS offers three types of load balancers: Application Load Balancer (ALB), Network Load Balancer (NLB), and Classic Load Balancer (CLB)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Application Load Balancer (ALB)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"ALB is best suited for load balancing of HTTP and HTTPS traffic. It operates at the application layer (layer 7 of the OSI model) and provides advanced routing capabilities, such as content-based routing, host-based routing, and path-based routing. ALB supports containerized applications and microservices."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Operates at Layer 7 (Application Layer)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket protocols."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides content-based routing (routing based on the content of the request)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports host-based routing (routing based on the hostname in the request)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports path-based routing (routing based on the URL path in the request)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integrates with AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports containerized applications (e.g., with ECS and EKS)."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"# Example ALB configuration (simplified)
resource "aws_lb" "example" {
  name               = "example-alb"
  internal           = false
  load_balancer_type = "application"
  security_groups    = ["sg-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"]
  subnets            = ["subnet-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "subnet-yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"]
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Network Load Balancer (NLB)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"NLB is best suited for load balancing of TCP, UDP, and TLS traffic where extreme performance and low latency are required. It operates at the transport layer (layer 4 of the OSI model) and is capable of handling millions of requests per second while maintaining ultra-low latencies. NLB is ideal for applications such as gaming, IoT, and streaming media."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Operates at Layer 4 (Transport Layer)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports TCP, UDP, and TLS protocols."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides extremely high performance and low latency."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handles millions of requests per second."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports static IP addresses per Availability Zone."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ideal for applications requiring high throughput and low latency."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"# Example NLB configuration (simplified)
resource "aws_lb" "example" {
  name               = "example-nlb"
  internal           = false
  load_balancer_type = "network"
  subnets            = ["subnet-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "subnet-yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"]
}"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Classic Load Balancer (CLB)"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"CLB is the previous generation load balancer. It provides basic load balancing across multiple EC2 instances and operates at both the request level and connection level. While still supported, it's recommended to use ALB or NLB for new applications due to their advanced features and performance."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supports HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, and SSL protocols."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Operates at both Layer 4 and Layer 7."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides basic load balancing functionality."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Less flexible and performant compared to ALB and NLB."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Recommended to use ALB or NLB for new applications."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"# Example CLB configuration (simplified)
resource "aws_elb" "example" {
  name               = "example-clb"
  availability_zones = ["us-west-2a", "us-west-2b"]
  listeners {
    instance_port     = 80
    instance_protocol = "http"
    lb_port           = 80
    lb_protocol       = "http"
  }
}"},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing the Right Load Balancer"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice of load balancer depends on your application's requirements. Use ALB for HTTP/HTTPS traffic and advanced routing. Use NLB for high-performance, low-latency applications using TCP, UDP, or TLS. Avoid using CLB for new applications; migrate to ALB or NLB if possible."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ALB: HTTP/HTTPS traffic, content-based routing, containerized applications."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NLB: TCP/UDP/TLS traffic, high performance, low latency."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"CLB: Legacy applications (consider migrating to ALB or NLB)."}]
